w11 labwc geometry shim -- native-toplevel geometry (AGENTS.md route 6)
======================================================================

Why this exists
---------------
On the wlr floor (labwc, and the labwc-backed Budgie / Xfce-on-Wayland /
LXQt-on-Wayland sessions) w11's window backend answers an XWayland window's real
rectangle over the X plane (route 5), but a NATIVE Wayland toplevel had no
rectangle at all: zwlr_foreign_toplevel_management_v1 and
ext_foreign_toplevel_list_v1 carry a title and an app id and no geometry, and no
X server has heard of the window.  So `wdotool getwindowgeometry` on a native
window answered `0,0 out_w x out_h` -- the whole output.

The rule (AGENTS.md): we never fork, patch or rebuild an upstream package.  The
distro's labwc and libwlroots stay bit-for-bit what the distro ships.  So the
lowest reachable rung for the labwc family is route 6 done from OUTSIDE: our own
code, in w11's own package, loaded into the stock compositor with LD_PRELOAD.
(An earlier attempt forked labwc and shipped a rebuilt labwc_0.9.3-1w11.1 .deb;
that is exactly what the rule now forbids, and this shim replaces it.)

The mechanism
-------------
labwc links libwlroots-0.19 dynamically, so wlroots' exported symbols are
interposable.  w11-labwc-shim.c interposes two of them and listens to one signal:

  * wlr_scene_xdg_surface_create(parent, xdg_surface) -- labwc builds every view's
    scene tree with this call, and wlroots guarantees the returned node's origin
    "will match the top-left corner of the xdg_surface window geometry".  That
    ties a scene node to an xdg_surface, which is the correlation labwc keeps in
    its own struct view and the hard part of doing this from outside.  We register
    each toplevel (popups skipped) and add a destroy listener to its scene node so
    the row goes when the view unmaps or closes.

  * wlr_scene_output_build_state(...) -- called once per output per composited
    frame, i.e. exactly when the scene changed.  We rebuild the file there and
    write only when its bytes differ from the last write (content-debounced), so a
    static desktop writes nothing and a move / resize / rename / map / unmap is on
    disk within one frame.  This is what makes the resize and rename cases correct
    with no request-specific hook.

At write time, per registered toplevel:
  * rectangle -- wlr_scene_node_coords(&tree->node, &x, &y) is the absolute
    on-screen top-left of the window geometry (== labwc's view->current.x/y), and
    xdg_surface->geometry.{width,height} is its size;
  * identity  -- xdg_surface->toplevel->title / ->app_id (the same strings labwc
    hands the foreign-toplevel handle, so the reader's join key is byte-identical);
  * pid       -- wl_client_get_credentials() of the surface's client.

The runtime contract (unchanged; the reader already expects it)
---------------------------------------------------------------
The shim writes $XDG_RUNTIME_DIR/w11-labwc-geometry, rewritten atomically
(temp + rename), one line per currently-displayed toplevel view, tab-separated:

    <pid>\t<x>\t<y>\t<w>\t<h>\t<app_id>\t<title>

x/y/w/h are the view's on-screen window-geometry rectangle -- the same rectangle
getwindowgeometry prints for an XWayland view on this floor.  Tabs, newlines and
carriage returns in app_id/title are replaced with spaces so the line format is
stable; title is the last field.  hacks/window/backend_wlr.py
(WlrBackend._labwc_geometry) folds the rect+pid onto the native rows by
(app_id, title); a key two windows share is dropped, so a tie keeps the floor.

Build
-----
build-shim.sh compiles w11-labwc-shim.c against the distro's libwlroots-0.19 dev
headers (Ubuntu 26.04: gcc, pkgconf, libwlroots-0.19-dev, libwayland-dev,
wayland-protocols):

    sh build-shim.sh                 # -> w11-labwc-shim.so beside the source

The struct offsets it reads are the 0.19.x ABI -- the same soname labwc's own
libwlroots-0.19.so exports -- so the compiled .so matches the running library.
This is a build-on-the-target step, not something baked into the Architecture:
all .deb: a .so for another release's wlroots would be the wrong ABI.  The w11
package builds it at install time (postinst, best-effort) into
/usr/lib/w11/w11-labwc-shim.so; the session wrapper (packaging/common/
w11-labwc-session) falls back to building it once into the user's cache.

Use / measure
-------------
The package installs a Wayland session entry (/usr/share/wayland-sessions/
w11-labwc.desktop) whose Exec is

    /usr/lib/w11/w11-labwc-session labwc

which preloads the shim and exec's the stock labwc.  Pick "labwc (w11)" at the
greeter, or point greetd's command at the wrapper.

Measured on the resolute-labwc golden (Ubuntu 26.04, labwc 0.9.3, libwlroots-0.19
0.19.2), 2026-09-15, a 1280x800 head:

    stock labwc:  wdotool getwindowgeometry -> Position: 0,0   Geometry: 1280x800
                  wdotool getwindowpid      -> window ... has no pid associated
    + the shim:   wdotool getwindowgeometry -> Position: 292,166 Geometry: 696x494
                  wdotool getwindowpid      -> 7790

The shim's file said `6354 292 166 696 494 foot foot`; a QMP screendump put the
foot's content at x=292, right edge 987, bottom 659 -- matching exactly (the top
sits 21 px below labwc's SSD titlebar, which the window geometry correctly
excludes, exactly as an XWayland getwindowgeometry does).  A foot that renamed
itself to `renamed-after-map` after map kept its rectangle and pid (the join key
tracks the live title); closing it removed the row; two identical `foot` windows
both kept the floor (the reader drops a shared key).

Coverage
--------
Budgie 10.10, Xfce 4.20-on-Wayland and LXQt-on-Wayland all run labwc 0.9.3 on the
same libwlroots-0.19, so the same .so and the same wrapper cover them: launch the
session's compositor through w11-labwc-session (the shim is inert in any process
that never calls wlr_scene_*, so a session-wide preload is harmless).  Plain
labwc is what was measured this pass; the other three are the same compositor and
the same code path, not separately measured here.

Upstreaming
-----------
The upstreamable form of the same emit is a foreign-toplevel protocol event
carrying the rectangle (cosmic's zcosmic_toplevel_info_v1 v2 `geometry` is the
matching shape, and labwc already ships cosmic-workspace).  That would make the
row AGENTS.md route 1 in a later release, with no shim and no runtime file.
